course: dictionary
lesson: 2399
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word curtain is a good example. Its main meaning is a piece of cloth that hangs over a window to block light or stop people looking in, as in «people draw the curtains at night».
explain: Curtain can also mean the big cloth across the front of a stage in a theatre. Look at the clues: «the curtain fell «whoosh!»» tells you we are watching a show, not sitting in a room.
explain: A curtain can also be a thick wall of something, as in «a curtain of cold air» that blows down the front of a supermarket fridge. Always swap your meaning into the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
fact: A curtain is a piece of cloth that hangs, often over a window, to block light or stop people looking in.
fact: In a theatre, the curtain is the big cloth across the front of the stage: it goes up at the start and comes down at the end.
fact: A curtain of something is a thick wall of it, like «a curtain of cold air» or a curtain of rain.
fact: A curtain call is when the actors come back on stage to bow after a show.
fact: Curtain cords can strangle young children, so keep them tied up high, and keep curtains away from candles and heaters.
exercise: «Open supermarket chillers blow a curtain of cold air down the front.» What does curtain mean here? | Cloth hanging over a window | A cold, wet blanket | *A thick wall of cold air | A stage cloth in a theatre
exercise: «A girl put her Christmas tree lights next to curtains; they overheated and started a small fire.» Why should curtains be kept away from lights and heaters? | *They can catch fire | They might look messy | They make the room too dark | They block the sunlight
exercise: «The curtain fell «whoosh!» and the magic began.» Which meaning of curtain is this? | A thick wall of rain | *The big cloth across the front of a stage | A cloth over a window | A shower curtain
test: What does a curtain call mean? | When the cloth over a window is pulled shut | When a thick wall of rain falls | *When actors come back to bow after a show | When someone calls the police
test: Mum says, «Please draw the curtains.» What does she want you to do? | *Pull the cloth across the window | Hang up your coat | Put the lights on the stage | Open the front door
exam: «Your suitcase disappears behind a rubber curtain at check-in.» What does curtain mean here? | The stage cloth in a theatre | A plastic bag that hides the case | A cloth you draw across a window | *A hanging wall of rubber strips that the case passes through
